  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Known as one of the Provence Landing sites (night of 14 to 15 August 1944, Commandos d'Afrique), Canadel beach is today a peaceful sandy beach, split into two zones by a lifeguard post. On the right, a stele commemorates the landing. It is reached on foot or by bike along the Voie Verte, or by car via a car park signposted on the D559, near the chapel (tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>From 1 June to 30 September, dogs on a leash are allowed on all the town's beaches between 7pm and 8am only; outside those hours, they are not allowed. This is what the town hall (news item of 23 June 2026 and the summer 2026 "Carnets du Rayol-Canadel-Pramousquier" bulletin) and the Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office state. Owners must pick up waste; identified guide and assistance dogs kept on a leash remain welcome. For the rest of the year (1 October to 31 May), neither the town nor the tourist office publishes a precise rule: ask the town hall before coming.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the town has removed the bins from the beaches ("no bins directly on the beaches", summer 2026 bulletin) — bring bags and leave with your dog's waste.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The nearby Domaine du Rayol botanical garden, however, does not admit pets, even on a leash or in a backpack (Domaine's rules) — worth knowing if you plan to stop there on the way. <a href="/en/fiche-plage-pramousquier-rayol-canadel">Pramousquier beach</a> is 2 km away along the Voie Verte.</p>
    </div>

